/E (For instance, in quantitative finance, Agentic AI systems might employ semi-supervised learning to analyze vast historical stock prices and market sentiment \(unlabeled news articles, social media feeds\). As new market data arrives, the agent continually updates its feature engineering pipelines and predictive models \(e.g., ensemble methods, deep neural networks\). When encountering novel market conditions \(e.g., high volatility event, regulatory change\), the system's adaptive algorithms \(e.g., Kalman filters for state estimation, adaptive resonance theory networks\) can dynamically adjust the model's weights or even restructure parts of its internal representation, ensuring that its predictions remain robust despite concept drift.)
